\chapter{Reproducible debugging and toolchain probes}
\label{ch:68}
\index{Reproducible debugging and toolchain probes}
\index{Debugging!DWARF paths}

Project debug builds retain debug information but can normalize selected paths using \OnxCodeTerm{-fdebug-prefix-map}. This maps paths recorded in DWARF, not actual filesystem locations. The debugger still needs access to the generated C and compatible runtime sources. A matching build helper invocation in emit-only mode can recreate the generated C for inspection.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
set substitute-path /__on1x__/generated /path/to/generated-c
set substitute-path /__on1x__/sdk /path/to/on1x-sdk
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Compare binaries across different checkout roots using the same compiler versions, assembler, linker, flags and sysroot. A path map cannot normalize toolchain-dependent code or strings that a program explicitly embeds. The toolchain probe documents which tools and target details can be collected. Preserve those results alongside the effective manifest and compiler revision. The resulting debugging view is of generated C; use source diagnostics and checked locations to relate findings back to On1x.

\section{Design a comparison that cannot pass by stripping}
The debug regression creates two copies of a project, each with its own
SDK and temporary root. In both, the entry imports a function returning
42. Keep the native compiler fixed within a pair. First execute each
result: printing 42 checks that the import and link worked, but says
nothing about path leakage. Next compare the complete executable bytes
and the build report bytes. Finally look for the debug information and
line-table sections, the normalized generated C filename, and the
absence of either checkout path. Removing all debug information could
satisfy the path-absence test while making source debugging useless;
the positive markers catch exactly that false success.

Suppose two binaries still differ after the virtual filenames match.
Repeat the pair under GCC, then separately under Clang. Equality within
each pair and inequality between compilers are consistent outcomes. If
a single pair differs, inspect program data as well as DWARF: mapping
debug paths does not rewrite a filename deliberately compiled into a C
string literal. Retain the unstripped pair so the location of a difference
can still be investigated.

For a different question---whether three selected tools work together---the
Linux x86-64 toolchain probe compiles a C layout fixture, assembles a
separate routine, links and executes the result. A good probe record
identifies the tools that passed that call boundary. It neither supplies
missing debugger sources nor pins every startup object and library used
by the linker. A relocation of the installation can change the probe's
canonical tool paths even when the same tool binaries pass every check.

